  "raw_label": "appellant_lost",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sri Kiran Kumar R vs Sri Raju Kadegowda on 15 October, 2024 -1- Nc: 2024:Khc:41724 Wp No. 26426 of 2024 In The High Court Of Karnataka At Bengaluru Dated This The 15Th Day Of October, 2024 Before The Hon'Ble Mr Justice R. Nataraj Writ Petition No. 26426 Of 2024 (Gm-Cpc) Between: Sri Kiran Kumar R., Aged About 27 Years, S/O Raju Kadegowda R/A No. 376, 1B Main Road, Vivekananda Park Back Gate, Girinagara, Bangalore-560085. ...Petitioner (By Sri. Chandranath Ariga K., Advocate) And: 1. Sri Raju Kadegowda Aged About 62 Years, R/A No.81, 3Rd Cross, Vinayaka Layout, Machohalli, Bangalore - 562162 Digitally signed 2. M/S Samrat Foods by Markonahalli Having Its Registered Office Ramu Priya Location: High Proprietorship Concern Court Of Karnataka Thotadaguddadahalli, Nagasandra (P) Tumkur Road, Bangalore North Bangalore-73. ...Respondents This Wp Is Filed Under Articles 226 And 227 Of The Constitution Of India Praying To [LEAKAGE_MASK] The Order Sri Kiran Kumar R vs Sri Raju Kadegowda on 15 October, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/146703325/ 1 Dated 17.09.2024 Passed On I.A No.Ii And Iii In Com O.S No.253/2024 On The File OF XI ADDL. DISTRICT AND SESSIONS JUDGE AT BANGALORE RURAL (COMMERCIAL COURT) (ANNEXURE-L) AND ETC. -2- NC: 2024:KHC:41724 WP No. 26426 of 2024 THIS PETITION, COMING ON FOR ORDERS, THIS DAY, ORDER WAS MADE THEREIN AS UNDER: CORAM : HON'BLE MR JUSTICE R. NATARAJ ORAL ORDER",
    "facts": "The defendant No.1 had obtained a licence from FSSAI to manufacture food products. He claimed that the factory was established on the suit 'A' and 'B' schedule properties. The defendant No.1 issued another notice dated 26.06.2024 calling upon the plaintiff to hand over the possession of the unit and the business with accounts. The plaintiff claims that he came to know that the defendant No.1 was running a factory in the name and style - M/s.Samrat foods to manufacture and sell products under the brand name 'SANGAVI FOODS', illegally. The defendant No.1 filed a suit in O.S. No.382/2024 on the file of III Additional Civil Judge and JMFC., Nelamangala for perpetual injunction in respect of the suit 'A' schedule property. I.A. No.II was filed for NC: 2024:KHC:41724 injunction to restrain the defendants from manufacturing and labelling products under the name 'SANGAVI FOODS' while I.A. No.III was filed for interim injunction to restrain the defendant No.1 from interfering with the business and management of 'SANGAVI FOODS' established in the suit schedule properties pending disposal of the suit. The Commercial Court after considering the said applications, issued emergent notice on I.A. Nos.II and III and suit summons to the defendants and did not grant ex parte injunction that was prayed by the plaintiff.",
    "arguments": "5. Learned counsel for the plaintiff vehemently contended that when once the business was handed over to the plaintiff under an agreement dated 01.10.2022, the defendant No.1 could not have indulged in any act to commit the breach of the agreement. He, therefore, contended that the Commercial Court ought to have granted ex parte interim injunction orders to ensure that the agreement dated 01.10.2022 is not breached. Besides this, he contended that the Commercial Court did not apply its mind to the facts and NC: 2024:KHC:41724 circumstances of the case before holding that the plaintiff had not made out a prima facie case for grant of ex parte injunction."
  },
